        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a walking pad during video calls?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, with some care. Slow to about 1 mph or stop when you speak, use a headset with a close microphone and noise suppression rather than a laptop microphone, put a dense mat under the pad to cut footfall, and keep the belt lubricated. The motor hum is rarely the problem; the thud of your steps usually is.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many hours a day can a walking pad run?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the model, and most listings do not say. Of the pads on this page, only the UREVO E4W states a figure, up to four hours of continuous use. When a listing gives no limit, plan on walking in blocks of 30 to 60 minutes with breaks, and check the manual.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk at while working?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most people type and take calls comfortably at around 1 to 2 mph. Start at the slowest speed you find natural and build up over a week or two. Fine mouse work is easier standing still on the stopped pad.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is an office treadmill the same as a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most office treadmills sold today are walking pads: low, slim treadmills without a console or handrail, designed to sit under a standing desk. Heavier-duty under-desk treadmills such as the LifeSpan TX6 are built for longer daily use but work the same way.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a walking pad at my workplace?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Ask first. Many employers have rules on personal electrical equipment, and facilities will want to know that the pad and its cable do not block walkways or exits. Plug it straight into a wall outlet, use a mat to reduce noise for neighbours, and choose a pad that folds or slides under the desk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How high does my desk need to go for a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Your standing elbow height in shoes plus the pad's deck height, typically 4 to 6 inches. For many adults that means a desk reaching 48 to 50 inches or more. Our standing desk height page has a chart by user height.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

